/** \addtogroup area_sequence */
/** \defgroup theory_sequence Theory of sequences and lists
 \ingroup area_sequence

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/sequence` realize the SEQUENCE of
 section 3.2.1 of the book and the LIST of chapter 4, i.e., a vector that
 doubles and halves (section 4.2), the bidirectional circular list with a
 sentinel (section 4.1), the singly linked list of exercise 4.6, the lists
 with cursors in one vector, and, beyond the book, a vector with room for a
 few items inside itself and the tiered vector of Goodrich and Kloss; next to
 them are the algorithms that work on a sequence through its positions (the
 searches, the natural merge sort, the merge) or on a vector in place (the
 partition into three classes and a few other rearrangements). This page
 collects the definitions and the notation that the headers share (see
 \ref theory_sequence_notation), the realizations with the costs of their
 operations (see \ref theory_sequence_repr), the amortized analysis of the
 vectors that change their size (see \ref theory_sequence_amortized), and,
 for the algorithms, the property on which each is correct and the argument
 behind its bound (see \ref theory_sequence_sort and
 \ref theory_sequence_rearr), and the lists by rank of chapters 2 and 3 of
 Morin's Open Data Structures with the answers to their exercises (see
 \ref theory_sequence_ods), and the sequences by rank on skip lists and
 treaps and the order maintenance of his chapters 4, 7 and 8 (see
 \ref theory_sequence_morin). The pseudocode of each operation and of each
 algorithm is on the page of its header, in the comment of its function,
 together with the section of the book, or the paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_sequence_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>sequence</em> \f$ s = s_0 , s_1 , \ldots , s_{n-1} \f$ is a finite
 list of \f$ n \ge 0 \f$ items, possibly repeated, in which the order matters;
 the SEQUENCE of the book (see \ref theory_ADS_adt, and the concept
 `Sequence` of `ADT.h`) reaches its items through <em>positions</em>, values
 of a type `Pos` that the realization chooses, rather than through indices.
 The position \f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$ leads to \f$ s_i \f$ for
 \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$, and two more positions, \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$ and
 \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$, stand before the first item and after the last one,
 so that a scan reads
 \f[
   p \gets \mathrm{head}();\ \textbf{while not}\ \mathrm{finished}( p )
   \ \textbf{do}\ \ldots\ \mathrm{read}( p ) \ldots ;\ p \gets \mathrm{next}( p
   ) . \tag{1}
 \f]
 What a position is decides the cost of each operation: an index costs
 nothing to move along but makes an insertion shift the items after it,
 while a pointer to a cell makes an insertion change two links but gives no
 access by index. A <em>run</em> of a sequence is a maximal stretch of
 consecutive items in nondecreasing order, and a sequence of \f$ r \f$ runs
 has \f$ r - 1 \f$ descents, i.e., places with \f$ s_{i+1} < s_i \f$.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ s_i \f$</td>
     <td>the number of items, and the item of index \f$ i \f$,
         \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$</td>
     <td>the position of \f$ s_i \f$, and \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$,
         \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ those before the first item and after the
         last one, on which <tt>finished()</tt> is true</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c \f$</td>
     <td>the capacity of a vector, the number of items it has room
         for</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \alpha = n / c \f$</td>
     <td>the load of a vector</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ \Phi \f$</td>
     <td>a potential, a function of the state of a structure that pays for
         its dear operations</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ b \f$</td>
     <td>the number of places of a block of the tiered vector, a power of
         2</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ r \f$</td>
     <td>the number of runs of a sequence</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$</td>
     <td>a vector of the C++ code, indexed from 0, and \f$ A[ i .. j ) \f$
         its places from \f$ i \f$ to \f$ j - 1 \f$</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_sequence_repr Realizations and their costs

 The library has six realizations of a sequence, four of which have the
 positions of the book and model `Sequence`, while the other two are vectors
 with access by index only.

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of a sequence and the cost of their operations
          (sections 4.1 and 4.2 of the book, exercise 4.6)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Position</th><th>Space</th>
     <th><tt>read()</tt>, <tt>next()</tt></th><th><tt>prev()</tt>,
     <tt>tail()</tt></th><th><tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt></th>
     <th>access by index</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArraySequence</tt></td><td>the index</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n - i ) \f$, \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
     amortized at the end</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>List</tt></td><td>the cell of the item</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( i ) \f$ by a scan</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SinglyList</tt></td><td>the cell before the item</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( i ) \f$ by a scan</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CursorList</tt></td><td>the cursor of the cell before the
     item</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, shared by the lists of a space</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ O( i ) \f$ by a scan</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SmallVector</tt></td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n + N ) \f$</td><td>none</td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized at the end</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TieredVector</tt></td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ n + O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$</td><td>none</td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$ amortized anywhere</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SkipListSequence</tt>, <tt>TreapList</tt></td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ expected</td><td>none</td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected anywhere</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected</td></tr>
 </table>

 An insertion in a vector at the index \f$ i \f$ moves the \f$ n - i \f$
 items after it one place on, and a removal moves them back; hence the
 vector is preferable when the sequence is mostly read, by index or in
 order, and changed at its end, and its items are contiguous, which makes a
 scan read the memory forward. A list changes in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time at any
 position it holds, since an insertion allocates a cell and changes the
 links of its two neighbors, but it has no access by index, and a scan
 follows pointers that may be scattered in memory; the cells of `List` and
 of `SinglyList` come therefore from a pool, so that the cells made one
 after another are next to each other.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the position before the item).</em> In a list whose cells
 have a link to the next one only, an insertion before \f$ s_i \f$ and the
 removal of \f$ s_i \f$ change the link of the cell of \f$ s_{i-1} \f$ (or
 the link to the first cell, for \f$ i = 0 \f$); with
 \f$ \mathit{pos}_i \f$ the cell of \f$ s_{i-1} \f$, and nil for
 \f$ i = 0 \f$, both take \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, while `prev()` and `tail()`
 take \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst case.

 Indeed, the link to change is the one that points to the cell of
 \f$ s_i \f$, which is in the cell before it, and no link leads from a cell
 back to its predecessor; a position that held the cell of \f$ s_i \f$ itself
 would force a scan from the head to find it, i.e., \f$ \Theta( i ) \f$
 time. With the position before the item, the scan is needed only to go
 backwards, i.e., by `prev()` and by `tail()`, which is the convention of the
 realizations with pointers and with cursors of Aho, Hopcroft and Ullman.
 `SinglyList` and `CursorList` follow it, with \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ the
 last cell; after `insert( p , v )` the new item has the position \f$ p \f$,
 and after `remove( p )` its successor has it. The bidirectional list
 `List` keeps instead both links in each cell and a sentinel, a cell with no
 item that is the predecessor of the first cell and the successor of the
 last one: every cell has then both neighbors, and the insertion and the
 removal have no special case for the empty list or for the ends, the
 sentinel being \f$ \mathit{pos}_{-1} \f$ and \f$ \mathit{pos}_n \f$ at once.

 A list with <em>cursors</em> (`CursorList`) keeps its cells in a vector,
 shared by several lists, the link of a cell being the index of the next one
 and 0 being nil; the free cells form one more list in the same vector, the
 free list, from which `take()` removes its first cell and to which `give()`
 returns a cell, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time. When the free list is empty the
 vector doubles, which costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized by Lemma 2 below, and
 the cursors stay valid, since an index does not change when the vector is
 moved, while a pointer would.

 \section theory_sequence_amortized Vectors that change their size

 A vector of capacity \f$ c \f$ holds \f$ n \le c \f$ items; when an
 insertion finds it full it is replaced by one of capacity \f$ 2 c \f$, and
 the \f$ n \f$ items are moved there, which costs \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$. The
 cost of a single insertion is therefore \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst
 case, but that of a sequence of operations is linear in their number.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the doubling, section 4.2.1).</em> Starting from the empty
 vector, \f$ n \f$ insertions at the end cost in all
 \f[
   C( n ) = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \le n + \sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor}
            2^k \le n + 2^{\lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor + 1} - 1 \le 3 n ,
   \tag{2}
 \f]
 with \f$ c_i = i \f$ when the \f$ i \f$-th insertion finds the vector full,
 i.e., when \f$ i - 1 \f$ is a power of 2, and \f$ c_i = 1 \f$ otherwise.

 The bound (2) is the sum of the moves of the doublings, each a power of 2
 up to \f$ n \f$, and of one write per insertion; hence an insertion at the
 end costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized. Growing the vector by a constant
 \f$ d \f$ instead of doubling it gives a resize every \f$ d \f$
 insertions, the \f$ k \f$-th one moving \f$ k d \f$ items, i.e.,
 \f$ \Theta( n^2 / d ) \f$ in all, which is quadratic.

 A vector that only grows keeps the space of its largest size. To keep the
 space \f$ O( n ) \f$, `ArraySequence` halves its capacity when a removal
 leaves it with \f$ n \le c / 4 \f$ items (halving it at \f$ n = c / 2 \f$
 would make an insertion and a removal around that size each cost
 \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$, a resize every time).

 <em>Lemma 3 (the doubling and the halving).</em> Any sequence of insertions
 and removals at the end of a vector that doubles when it is full and halves
 when it is a quarter full costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized time per operation.

 Indeed, let the potential be
 \f[
   \Phi = \begin{cases} 2 n - c & \text{if } \alpha \ge 1 / 2 , \cr
                        c / 2 - n & \text{if } \alpha < 1 / 2 ,
          \end{cases} \tag{3}
 \f]
 which is never negative and is 0 when the vector is half full. An operation
 that does not resize changes \f$ n \f$ by 1 and \f$ \Phi \f$ by at most 2,
 i.e., its amortized cost, its cost plus the change of \f$ \Phi \f$, is at
 most 3. A doubling happens at \f$ n = c \f$, where \f$ \Phi = n \f$, and a
 halving at \f$ n = c / 4 \f$, where \f$ \Phi = c / 4 = n \f$: in both cases
 the \f$ n \f$ moves are paid by the potential, which falls to \f$ O( 1 ) \f$
 (Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest and Stein, section 17.4, after Tarjan).
 `CursorSpace` grows in the same way, and `SmallVector` doubles once its
 \f$ N \f$ inner places are full, so that \f$ n \f$ insertions move fewer than
 \f$ 2 n \f$ items and the space is at most \f$ 2 n + N \f$ items.

 The tiered vector of Goodrich and Kloss trades the access in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ of a vector for an insertion and a removal at any index in
 \f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$. Its items are in blocks of \f$ b \f$ places, each a
 circular buffer with the offset of its first item, all full but the last
 one; the item of index \f$ i \f$ is the item \f$ i \bmod b \f$ of the block
 \f$ \lfloor i / b \rfloor \f$, i.e., with \f$ b \f$ a power of 2, a shift
 and a mask, and inside the block it is at the place
 \f$ ( \mathit{off} + i ) \bmod b \f$.

 <em>Lemma 4 (the tiered vector).</em> An insertion or a removal at the index
 \f$ i \f$ moves \f$ O( b + n / b ) \f$ items, i.e., \f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$
 with \f$ b = \Theta( \sqrt{n} ) \f$, and keeping \f$ b \f$ the least power of
 2 not below \f$ \sqrt{n} \f$, by a rebuild in \f$ O( n ) \f$ when \f$ n \f$
 leaves \f$ [ b^2 / 4 , 4 b^2 ] \f$, costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized per
 operation.

 Indeed, an insertion moves by one place the items of its block after
 \f$ i \f$, fewer than \f$ b \f$, and then, from the last block back to the
 block after that of \f$ i \f$, the last item of each block to the front of
 the next one; the front of a circular buffer moves by decreasing its
 offset, i.e., in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and there are at most \f$ n / b + 1 \f$
 blocks. A removal does the converse. Right after a rebuild \f$ b \f$ is
 the least power of 2 with \f$ b^2 \ge n \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ b^2 / 4 < n \le b^2 \f$, and the rebuilds that \f$ n \f$ crossing a
 bound triggers leave \f$ n \f$ about \f$ b^2 \f$ for the new \f$ b \f$; the
 next rebuild needs \f$ n \f$ to grow to \f$ 4 b^2 \f$ or to fall to
 \f$ b^2 / 4 \f$, i.e., \f$ \Omega( n ) \f$ operations, which pay for its
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ moves. The space is that of the \f$ n \f$ items and of at
 most one block not full, i.e., \f$ n + O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$.

 \section theory_sequence_sort Searching, merging and sorting a sequence

 The algorithms of `SequenceAlgorithms.h` use only the operations of the
 SEQUENCE, so that they run on every realization, with the cost of a scan
 (1), i.e., \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ calls of `next()` and `read()`. A search for
 an item compares it with the items in order, \f$ n \f$ comparisons when it
 misses; on a sorted sequence it may stop at the first larger item, but
 the comparisons stay \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ in the worst case, since the
 positions give no access in the middle (the binary search of the vectors
 needs the access by index).
 The merge of two sorted sequences of lengths \f$ n_1 \f$ and \f$ n_2 \f$
 appends at each step the smaller of their first items not yet taken, the
 one of the first sequence among equals, i.e., it is stable and makes at most
 \f$ n_1 + n_2 - 1 \f$ comparisons.

 <em>Lemma 5 (the natural merge sort).</em> A pass that distributes the
 runs of a sequence alternately to two sequences and merges them in pairs
 leaves at most \f$ \lceil r / 2 \rceil \f$ runs; hence the sort of a
 sequence of \f$ r \f$ runs makes \f$ \lceil \log_2 r \rceil \f$ passes and
 costs \f$ O( n \log r ) \f$, i.e., \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ in the worst case
 and \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ on a sorted sequence, which one scan finds to be a
 single run.

 Indeed, the merge of two sorted runs is a sorted stretch, and two runs that
 go to the same sequence one after another may only join into one; the
 \f$ j \f$-th pair of the merge therefore covers the runs \f$ 2 j - 1 \f$ and
 \f$ 2 j \f$ of the pass, and gives one run or part of one. Each pass costs
 \f$ \Theta( n ) \f$ reads and writes. The bound adapts to the order already
 present, as \f$ \log r \f$ falls from \f$ \log n \f$ to 0. It is also
 optimal up to a constant factor in the worst case for a given \f$ r \f$:
 the permutations whose runs have the lengths
 \f$ \ell_1 , \ldots , \ell_r \f$ are at most
 \f$ n! / ( \ell_1! \cdots \ell_r! ) \f$, whose logarithm is at most
 \f$ n \log_2 r \f$ and is \f$ \Omega( n \log r ) \f$ when the runs are
 of equal lengths, and a sort by comparisons must tell them apart (McIlroy).
 The bubble sort, instead, swaps
 adjacent items out of order until a pass has no swap; each swap removes one
 inversion, and the passes are at most \f$ n \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ comparisons and swaps.

 `listSort()` of `ListSort.h` sorts a `List` by relinking its cells, so
 that no item is copied and the positions stay on their items. It cuts the
 list into its runs, reversing those strictly decreasing (strictly, so that
 the sort stays stable), and merges them as a binary counter: the place
 \f$ k \f$ of an array holds the merge of \f$ 2^k \f$ runs or nothing, and a
 new run is merged with the places \f$ 0 , 1 , \ldots \f$ while they are
 full, as a carry propagates. Each run takes part in at most
 \f$ \lceil \log_2 r \rceil + 1 \f$ merges, which gives
 \f$ O( n \log r ) \f$ comparisons and \f$ n - 1 \f$ on a sorted or a
 reversed list, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ space beyond the array of 64 places. On
 the numbers in their natural order it uses instead the radix sort on the
 pairs (key, cell), in \f$ O( n ) \f$ time and space, since a merge sort
 walks the scattered cells \f$ \log r \f$ times and misses the cache at each
 of them.

 \section theory_sequence_rearr Rearrangements in place

 The functions of `Rearrangements.h` reorder a vector \f$ A[ 0 .. n - 1 ] \f$
 by swaps, or with a buffer much smaller than \f$ n \f$.

 <em>Lemma 6 (the Dutch national flag, Dijkstra).</em> With the indices
 \f$ i \le j \le k \f$, the invariant
 \f[
   A[ 0 .. i ) \text{ of color 0} , \quad A[ i .. j ) \text{ of color 1} ,
   \quad A[ j .. k ) \text{ not examined} , \quad A[ k .. n )
   \text{ of color 2} \tag{4}
 \f]
 holds at the start with \f$ i = j = 0 \f$ and \f$ k = n \f$ and is kept by
 a step that examines \f$ A[ j ] \f$; hence the items are partitioned by
 color after \f$ n \f$ steps, with \f$ n \f$ reads of a color and at most
 \f$ n \f$ swaps.

 Indeed, an item of color 0 is swapped with \f$ A[ i ] \f$, which is of
 color 1 (or is \f$ A[ j ] \f$ itself), and both \f$ i \f$ and \f$ j \f$
 grow; one of color 1 makes \f$ j \f$ grow; one of color 2 is swapped with
 \f$ A[ k - 1 ] \f$, not examined yet, and \f$ k \f$ falls. Each step makes
 the stretch not examined one shorter, and swaps at most once. It is the
 partition of the quicksort with three ways, the keys smaller than, equal to
 and larger than the pivot being the three colors.

 The alternating order \f$ A[ 0 ] \le A[ 1 ] \ge A[ 2 ] \le A[ 3 ] \ge \cdots
 \f$ takes one pass of \f$ n - 1 \f$ comparisons (`wiggleSort()`): if
 \f$ A[ 0 .. i ] \f$ alternates and \f$ A[ i ] \f$ and \f$ A[ i + 1 ] \f$ are
 in the wrong order, their swap keeps the order of \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \f$ and
 \f$ A[ i ] \f$, by transitivity (e.g., for an odd \f$ i \f$ with
 \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \le A[ i ] < A[ i + 1 ] \f$, the swap puts at the place
 \f$ i \f$ the larger \f$ A[ i + 1 ] \f$, which is still at least
 \f$ A[ i - 1 ] \f$).

 A vector is \f$ k \f$-<em>sorted</em> when each item is at most \f$ k \f$
 places away from its place in the sorted order; then the item of the place
 \f$ i \f$ of the sorted order is the least of those left among
 \f$ A[ i .. i + k ] \f$, and `sortKSorted()` keeps them in a heap of
 \f$ k + 1 \f$ items, which gives each item in \f$ O( \log k ) \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n \log k ) \f$ in all against the \f$ O( n k ) \f$ of the insertion
 sort. No sort by comparisons does better: the \f$ k \f$-sorted orders of
 \f$ n \f$ items are at least \f$ ( k! )^{n / k} \f$ (the blocks of \f$ k \f$
 places permuted independently), and a decision tree with that many leaves
 has a height of at least
 \f$ \log_2 ( k! )^{n / k} = \Omega( n \log k ) \f$.

 Finally, the permutation that sorts distinct items splits into cycles, and
 a swap that puts one item in its place shortens its cycle by one; a cycle
 of \f$ k > 1 \f$ items needs therefore \f$ k - 1 \f$ swaps, and the fewest
 swaps that sort the vector are \f$ n \f$ minus the number of cycles
 (`minimumSwapSort()`). When a swap costs the sum of its two items, a cycle
 of \f$ k \f$ items of sum \f$ s \f$ and least item \f$ m \f$ is sorted at
 the least cost
 \f[
   \min\{ s + ( k - 2 ) m , \ s + m + ( k + 1 ) M \} , \tag{5}
 \f]
 \f$ M \f$ the least item of the vector: either \f$ m \f$ is swapped in turn
 with each other item of the cycle, which takes part in one swap while
 \f$ m \f$ takes part in \f$ k - 1 \f$, or \f$ M \f$ enters the cycle by a
 swap with \f$ m \f$, does the same \f$ k - 1 \f$ swaps, and is swapped back
 (Knuth, section 1.3.3, on the cycles of a permutation).

 \section theory_sequence_ods Lists by rank (Morin's book)

 The interface LIST of chapter 1 of Morin's book reaches the items by their
 <em>rank</em>, \f$ \mathrm{get}( i ) \f$, \f$ \mathrm{set}( i , x ) \f$,
 \f$ \mathrm{add}( i , x ) \f$ and \f$ \mathrm{remove}( i ) \f$ for
 \f$ 0 \le i < n \f$ (\f$ i \le n \f$ for \f$ \mathrm{add} \f$), rather than
 by positions; the concept `IndexedList` of `IndexedList.h` states it with
 the names of the standard library (`operator[]`, `insert( i , x )`,
 `erase( i )`), and `IndexedDeque` states the DEQUE, the sequence changed
 at its two ends. The table gives the realizations of chapters 2 and 3 of
 Morin and of their exercises; \f$ \mu = \min\{ i , n - i \} \f$ is the
 distance of the rank from the nearer end.

 <table>
 <caption>The lists by rank and the cost of their operations, amortized
          where the realization rebuilds, \f$ \mu = \min\{ i , n - i \} \f$
          (chapters 2 and 3 of Morin's book)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Morin</th><th><tt>operator[]</tt></th>
     <th><tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>erase()</tt></th><th>space beyond
     \f$ n \f$ items</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArraySequence</tt></td><td><tt>ArrayStack</tt>,
     <tt>FastArrayStack</tt> (2.1, 2.2)</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 + n - i ) \f$</td><td>\f$ \le 3 n \f$ places</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RingDeque</tt></td><td><tt>ArrayQueue</tt>,
     <tt>ArrayDeque</tt> (2.3, 2.4)</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu ) \f$</td><td>\f$ < n \f$ places, until
     <tt>shrink_to_fit()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>DualArrayDeque</tt></td><td><tt>DualArrayDeque</tt> (2.5)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu ) \f$</td>
     <td>those of its two stacks</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>RootishArrayStack</tt></td><td><tt>RootishArrayStack</tt>
     (2.6)</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + n - i ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>DualArrayDeque</tt> on <tt>RootishArrayStack</tt></td>
     <td>exercise 2.10</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Treque</tt></td><td>exercise 2.4</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 + \min\{ \mu , | n / 2 - i | \} ) \f$</td>
     <td>those of two <tt>RingDeque</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ContiguousDeque</tt></td><td>exercise 2.9</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ \le 8 n + 24 \f$ places</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>TieredVector</tt></td><td>exercises 2.11, 2.12</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \min\{ \mu , \sqrt{n} \} ) \f$
     </td><td>\f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CubishArrayStack</tt></td><td>exercise 2.13</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( n^{1/3} ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( n^{2/3} ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SinglyList</tt></td><td><tt>SLList</tt> (3.1)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 + i ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + i ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ n \f$ pointers</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>List</tt></td><td><tt>DLList</tt> (3.2)</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ 2 n \f$ pointers</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SEList</tt></td><td><tt>SEList</tt> (3.3), exercises 3.17,
     3.18</td><td>\f$ O( 1 + \mu / b ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( b + \mu / b ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( b + n / b ) \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>XorList</tt></td><td>section 3.4</td><td>none</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ at the ends</td><td>\f$ n \f$ words</td></tr>
 </table>

 The rest of this section gives the argument behind each bound that is not
 the one of a shift of \f$ n - i \f$ items, and, for the exercises of the
 three chapters that ask for a proof or a discussion, the answer.

 \subsection theory_sequence_ods_arrays Deques on arrays

 `ArraySequence` is the `ArrayStack` of Morin: Lemma 2 above is the lemma of
 his section 2.1.2, with the halving at a quarter where Morin halves at a
 third, which changes the constant only. Exercise 2.1 asks where the extra item
 of the array is after a removal: in the place \f$ n \f$, the item that the
 shift moved out of it, which in C++ is the husk left by a move, alive until an
 insertion or a resize overwrites it; it keeps its resources only if its move
 does not take them (a `std::string` or a `std::vector` gives them away).
 Exercise 2.2 asks for `addAll( i , c )`: \f$ k \f$ calls of
 \f$ \mathrm{add}( i , x ) \f$ move the \f$ n - i \f$ items after \f$ i \f$
 \f$ k \f$ times, \f$ \Theta( k ( n - i ) ) \f$, while one shift by \f$ k \f$
 places after one resize moves them once, \f$ O( n - i + k ) \f$ (the range
 `insert()` of `ArraySequence`).

 `RingDeque` is the `ArrayQueue` and the `ArrayDeque` of Morin: the items in
 consecutive places of a circular vector, an insertion at the rank \f$ i \f$
 moving the \f$ \min\{ i , n - i \} \f$ items of the shorter side by one
 place. The capacity is a power of 2, so that the place of the rank
 \f$ i \f$ is \f$ ( h + i ) \mathbin{\&} ( m - 1 ) \f$ (exercise 2.8: the
 bitwise and in place of a division), and the shifts move the runs of
 items contiguous in the vector, three at most, by `std::move()`, i.e., by
 `memmove()` for the items trivially copyable (exercise 2.7, the
 `System.arraycopy()` of the Java edition). `rotate( r )` moves
 \f$ \min\{ r , n - r \} \f$ items from one end to the other, and none when
 the vector is full, where moving the head is enough (exercise 2.6).

 <em>Lemma 7 (the balance of two stacks, section 2.5.1 of Morin).</em> Let the
 `DualArrayDeque` hold its \f$ n \f$ items in two stacks \f$ F \f$ and
 \f$ B \f$, and deal them again into \f$ \lfloor n / 2 \rfloor \f$ and
 \f$ \lceil n / 2 \rceil \f$ items whenever \f$ 3 |F| < |B| \f$ or
 \f$ 3 |B| < |F| \f$. Then \f$ m \ge 1 \f$ operations from the empty deque
 spend \f$ O( m ) \f$ time in the balances, and an operation at the rank
 \f$ i \f$ shifts \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ i , n - i \} ) \f$ items.

 Indeed, after a balance of \f$ n \f$ items the two stacks differ by one
 item at most, and an operation changes \f$ |F| - |B| \f$ by one, while the
 next balance needs \f$ \max \ge 3 \min \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ | |F| - |B| | \ge ( n' - 2 ) / 2 \f$ for its \f$ n' \f$ items: at least
 \f$ n' / 2 - 2 \f$ operations, which pay for its \f$ O( n' ) \f$. Between
 two balances \f$ |F| \ge n / 4 \f$ and \f$ |B| \ge n / 4 \f$, so that a
 rank \f$ i < |F| \f$ is at \f$ |F| - i \le 3 i + 3 \f$ places from the top
 of \f$ F \f$ when \f$ i \le n / 4 \f$, and at
 \f$ |F| - i \le n \le 4 ( n - i ) \f$ otherwise, and the same for
 \f$ B \f$.

 The `Treque` of exercise 2.4 splits the items in the middle instead, into
 two circular deques \f$ L \f$ and \f$ R \f$ whose sizes differ by one at
 most, kept so by one item moved from the back of one to the front of the
 other after each operation: an operation at \f$ i < |L| \f$ shifts
 \f$ \min\{ i , |L| - i \} \f$ items, i.e., \f$ \min\{ i , n / 2 - i \} \f$
 up to a constant, and one at \f$ i \ge |L| \f$ shifts
 \f$ \min\{ i - n / 2 , n - i \} \f$, together
 \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ i , n - i , | n / 2 - i | \} ) \f$ with no amortization
 but that of the doublings.

 The `ContiguousDeque` of exercise 2.9 keeps the items in one contiguous
 piece \f$ A[ h .. h + n - 1 ] \f$ of a vector of \f$ c \f$ places, with no
 modular arithmetic; when an operation needs a place beyond an end, the
 items are put in the middle of \f$ 3 n + 8 \f$ places, at least
 \f$ n + 4 \f$ free on each side, so that at least \f$ n + 4 \f$ operations
 come before the next rebuild, which pays its \f$ O( n ) \f$ (the hint of the
 exercise asks for \f$ n / 2 \f$). A rebuild after a removal that leaves
 more than \f$ 9 n + 24 \f$ places keeps the space linear, and a rebuild in
 the same vector, by `memmove()`, costs no allocation when the vector is
 already of the right size, the common case of a queue that runs along it.

 \subsection theory_sequence_ods_rootish Rootish, tiered and cubish arrays

 The `RootishArrayStack` of Brodnik, Carlsson, Demaine, Munro and Sedgewick
 keeps its items in \f$ r \f$ blocks of \f$ 1 , \ldots , r \f$ places, the
 rank \f$ i \f$ in the block
 \f[
   b( i ) = \Bigl\lceil \frac{ -3 + \sqrt{ 9 + 8 i } }{ 2 } \Bigr\rceil \; ,
   \tag{6}
 \f]
 the least \f$ b \f$ with \f$ ( b + 1 ) ( b + 2 ) / 2 > i \f$, at the place
 \f$ i - b ( b + 1 ) / 2 \f$ of it.

 <em>Lemma 8 (the space of the rootish array, section 2.6.2 of Morin).</em> A
 `RootishArrayStack` that adds a block when its \f$ r ( r + 1 ) / 2 \f$ places
 are full and frees the last one while \f$ ( r - 2 ) ( r - 1 ) / 2 \ge n \f$
 has \f$ ( r - 2 ) ( r - 1 ) / 2 < n \le r ( r + 1 ) / 2 \f$, hence
 \f$ r \le \sqrt{ 2 n } + 2 \f$ and at most
 \f$ 2 r - 1 \le \sqrt{ 8 n } + 3 \f$ places unused; no array of \f$ n \f$
 items that grows and shrinks by one item in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time can waste
 \f$ o( \sqrt{n} ) \f$ places.

 Indeed, the places of the blocks are \f$ r ( r + 1 ) / 2 \f$ and
 \f$ n > ( r - 2 ) ( r - 1 ) / 2 \f$, whose difference is \f$ 2 r - 1 \f$;
 the lower bound is that of Brodnik et al., who show that after the
 insertion of \f$ n \f$ items some sequence of operations must leave
 \f$ \Omega( \sqrt{n} ) \f$ places wasted or allocated and not yet used.
 The blocks added and freed cost \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized, as in Lemma 2,
 since one block of \f$ r \f$ places is added or freed after \f$ r - 1 \f$
 operations at least. The test `ods-2.6.rootish-array-stack` checks the
 bound \f$ 2 r + 1 \f$ (the block kept for the next insertions included)
 after every operation of \f$ 10^5 \f$ insertions and removals.

 Section 2.6.4 of Morin computes (6) with no floating point, by a table of
 the square roots of the integers below \f$ 2^{16} \f$ and a table of the
 logarithms; `RootishArrayStack::block()` takes the square root of a
 `double`, exact on the integers below \f$ 2^{52} \f$, and corrects the
 result by the two comparisons with \f$ b ( b + 1 ) / 2 \f$ and
 \f$ ( b + 1 ) ( b + 2 ) / 2 \f$, which also absorb the rounding of the
 root (Morin's code computes \f$ 9 + 8 i \f$ in an `int`, which overflows
 for \f$ i > 2^{28} \f$). The tables cost two reads that may miss the
 cache, the root of a `double` a latency of about 15 to 20 cycles on the
 current processors, and the choice is left to the benchmarks.

 A `DualArrayDeque` whose two stacks are `RootishArrayStack` (exercise
 2.10) wastes \f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$ places in each stack, and shifts
 \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ i , n - i \} ) \f$ items by Lemma 7; a balance holds the
 items twice while it lasts.

 <em>Lemma 9 (the tiered vector with a gap, exercises 2.11 and 2.12).</em>
 Let a `TieredVector` keep its items in blocks of \f$ b \f$ places,
 \f$ b^2 / 4 \le n \le 4 b^2 \f$, all full but the first, with
 \f$ g < b \f$ places free at its front, and the last; let an insertion or a
 removal at the rank \f$ i \f$ shift toward the back when \f$ i \ge n / 2 \f$
 and toward the front otherwise. Then it moves
 \f[
   O\bigl( 1 + \min\{ \mu , b \} + \mu / b \bigr)
   = O\bigl( 1 + \min\{ i , \sqrt{n} , n - i \} \bigr) \tag{7}
 \f]
 items, \f$ \mu = \min\{ i , n - i \} \f$, plus \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized for
 the blocks added and removed and for the rebuilds when \f$ b \f$ changes,
 and wastes \f$ O( \sqrt{n} ) \f$ places.

 Indeed, a shift toward the back moves the items of the block of \f$ i \f$
 after \f$ i \f$, at most \f$ \min\{ b , n - i \} \f$, and then one item
 between each two consecutive blocks after it, at most
 \f$ ( n - i ) / b + 1 \f$ of them, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ each since a block is
 circular; the shift toward the front is the mirror, the gap of the first
 block playing the part of the free places of the last one, so that
 \f$ \min\{ b , i \} + i / b + 1 \f$ items move. A block is added at the
 front only when the gap is 0, after which \f$ b \f$ insertions at the
 front come before the next one, and inserting the pointer to it in front
 of the \f$ n / b \f$ others costs \f$ O( n / b ) = O( b ) \f$, i.e.,
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized. The bound of exercise 2.11,
 \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ \sqrt{n} , n - i \} ) \f$, is (7) without the shifts
 toward the front.

 <em>Lemma 10 (the cubish array, exercise 2.13).</em> Let
 `CubishArrayStack` keep its items in leaves of \f$ b \f$ places,
 \f$ b^3 / 8 \le n \le 8 b^3 \f$, gathered into middle blocks of
 \f$ b^2 \f$ items, all full but the last, each middle block being a
 circular sequence of leaves all full but the first (with a gap) and the
 last. Then `operator[]` takes \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, an insertion or a
 removal \f$ O( n^{1/3} ) \f$ amortized time, and the places wasted are
 \f$ O( n^{2/3} ) \f$.

 Indeed, a middle block pushes an item at its front and pops one from its
 back in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time, by its gap and the offset of its last leaf,
 as the first and the last block of Lemma 9 do; an insertion moves the last
 item of each full middle block after the one of \f$ i \f$ to the front of
 the next one, \f$ O( n / b^2 + 1 ) = O( b ) \f$ of them, and inserts in
 its middle block as a tiered vector of \f$ b \f$ leaves does,
 \f$ O( b ) \f$ by Lemma 9. Each middle block wastes the free places of
 its first and its last leaf and has a directory of \f$ 2 b \f$ leaves,
 \f$ O( b ) \f$ for each of \f$ O( n / b^2 + 1 ) \f$ middle blocks, i.e.,
 \f$ O( n / b ) = O( n^{2/3} ) \f$; the rebuilds, when \f$ n \f$ leaves
 \f$ [ b^3 / 8 , 8 b^3 ] \f$, cost \f$ O( n ) \f$ once every
 \f$ \Omega( n ) \f$ operations. This is the tiered vector of Goodrich and
 Kloss with three levels; the deque of middle blocks makes the two
 operations at the ends of a middle block cost \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, which the
 cascade of an insertion needs.

 \subsection theory_sequence_ods_linked Linked lists

 `SinglyList` is the `SLList` of Morin: it keeps its last cell and its
 size, so that it is a stack at its front and a queue from its back to its
 front in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time. A dummy cell before the first one cannot
 remove the special cases of these operations (exercise 3.1): the queue
 removes at the front and adds at the back, and the back is the last cell,
 which must be set apart from the dummy when the list is empty, while a
 removal at the back would need the cell before the last one, which a
 singly linked list reaches only by a scan. `secondLast()` (exercise 3.2)
 stops at the cell whose successor has no successor, with no use of the
 size; the operations by rank of exercise 3.3 walk \f$ i \f$ cells;
 `reverse()` (exercise 3.4) turns each link back while it walks, three
 pointers and no cell made; `checkSize()` (exercise 3.5) counts the cells.

 `List` is the `DLList` of Morin: a cell of rank \f$ i \f$ is reached from
 the sentinel by the nearer end, \f$ \min\{ i , n - i \} \f$ links
 (`position()`, Morin's `getNode()`). `rotate( r )` (exercise 3.8) moves
 the sentinel before the cell of rank \f$ n - r \f$, so that this cell is
 the first one, in \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ r , n - r \} ) \f$ and with no item
 moved; `truncate( i )` (exercise 3.9) cuts the chain before the cell of
 rank \f$ i \f$ and hangs it on the sentinel of a new list, in
 \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ i , n - i \} ) \f$; `absorb()` (exercise 3.10) links
 the chain of the other list after the last cell, in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$;
 `deal()` (exercise 3.11) relinks every second cell into a new list;
 `reverse()` (exercise 3.12) exchanges the two links of each cell and of
 the sentinel. These operations move cells between lists, which a list of
 cells from `new` does as Morin's does; the cells of a pooled list belong
 to its pool [see `ObjectPool.h`], so that `truncate()`, `deal()` and
 `takeFirst()` move the items into cells of the other list, while
 `absorb()` takes the pool of the other list together with its cells
 (`ObjectPool::adopt()`, which walks the \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ blocks of a
 pool).

 <em>Lemma 11 (the merge sort of a list, exercise 3.13).</em> The merge
 sort that cuts a list of \f$ n \f$ items in two halves by a walk to its
 middle, sorts the two recursively and merges them by `takeFirst()` is
 stable and takes \f$ O( n \log n ) \f$ time and \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ space.

 Indeed, the walk and the merge of a list of \f$ n \f$ items take
 \f$ O( n ) \f$, so that \f$ T( n ) = 2 T( n / 2 ) + O( n ) \f$, and the
 merge takes the item of the first half among equal ones, which keeps
 their order; the recursion is \f$ \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$ deep.
 `List::mergeSort()` runs it on the chain of the cells of the list, the
 halves cut by nil links and no list made, so that it relinks the cells of
 a pooled list as well; `listSort()` [see `ListSort.h`] is the bottom up
 natural merge sort, with \f$ n - 1 \f$ comparisons on a sorted list.

 \subsection theory_sequence_ods_selist Space-efficient lists

 The `SEList` of Morin keeps its items in a doubly linked list of blocks,
 each a circular deque of at most \f$ b + 1 \f$ items, the blocks between
 the first and the last holding at least \f$ b - 1 \f$ items each; hence
 \f$ n \f$ items take \f$ n / ( b - 1 ) + 2 \f$ blocks at most, i.e.,
 \f$ n + O( b + n / b ) \f$ places and \f$ O( n / b ) \f$ pointers, and the
 block of the rank \f$ i \f$ is reached from the nearer end through
 \f$ O( 1 + \min\{ i , n - i \} / b ) \f$ blocks.

 <em>Lemma 12 (spreading and gathering, section 3.3.5 of Morin).</em> Starting
 from the empty list, \f$ m \f$ insertions and removals take
 \f$ O( b m ) \f$ time in their calls of `spread()` and `gather()`.

 Indeed, let a middle block be <em>tight</em> when it holds \f$ b - 1 \f$
 or \f$ b + 1 \f$ items, and let \f$ \Phi \f$ be \f$ b \f$ times the number
 of tight blocks. An insertion or a removal that does not spread nor
 gather changes the size of one block by one and moves one item between
 neighbors, so that it makes at most one block tight, and raises
 \f$ \Phi \f$ by \f$ b \f$ at most; a spread is called on \f$ b \f$ blocks of
 \f$ b + 1 \f$ items and leaves \f$ b + 1 \f$ blocks of \f$ b \f$ items, and a
 gather on \f$ b \f$ blocks of \f$ b - 1 \f$ items (one of \f$ b - 2 \f$)
 and leaves \f$ b - 1 \f$ blocks of \f$ b \f$ items (two of \f$ b - 1 \f$ at
 most), so that each lowers \f$ \Phi \f$ by \f$ b ( b - 2 ) \f$ at least
 and pays its \f$ O( b^2 ) \f$.

 Unlike Morin's, the first and the last block of `SEList` hold from 1 to
 \f$ b + 1 \f$ items: an operation at an end adds a block when its block is
 full and drops it when it is empty, and never spreads nor gathers, so that
 the operations of a deque take \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time independent of \f$ b \f$
 (exercise 3.18), and those of a stack (exercise 3.17) a fortiori. With
 Morin's invariant, where only the last block is free, the stack takes
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized time as well: a push on a full last block walks
 one block to the dummy and adds a block, and a pop that leaves the last
 block with fewer than \f$ b - 1 \f$ items borrows nothing, so that a
 sequence of pushes and pops never spreads nor gathers; the deque needs the
 relaxation at the front too, since a removal at the front of Morin's list
 shifts one item out of each of the blocks that follow, up to \f$ b \f$ of
 them.

 \subsection theory_sequence_ods_xor XOR lists

 The discussion of chapter 3 of Morin mentions the XOR list, whose cell
 keeps \f$ \mathit{prev} \oplus \mathit{next} \f$, the exclusive or of the
 addresses of its neighbors: a walk that knows where it comes from finds
 where it goes by one exclusive or, both ways, since
 \f$ ( \mathit{prev} \oplus \mathit{next} ) \oplus \mathit{prev} =
 \mathit{next} \f$, and the list reversed is the same list read from its
 other end, which `XorList::reverse()` does in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$. The same
 identity, \f$ x \oplus x = 0 \f$ with \f$ \oplus \f$ associative and
 commutative, swaps two integers with no third (exercise 3.19,
 `xorSwap()`), except when both are the same variable, which the three
 exclusive ors set to 0.

 \subsection theory_sequence_ods_interfaces Interfaces and rotations

 Exercise 1.6 asks for reference realizations of LIST, USET and SSET on an
 array, the oracles of the tests: `ArraySequence` for the first and the
 `ArrayUSet` and `ArraySSet` of `SetInterfaces.h` for the others (see
 \ref theory_set). Exercise 1.7 asks how to make them faster: the shifts
 of a list by block moves and on the shorter side (`RingDeque`), the
 `find()` of an SSET by binary search on the sorted array, and its
 insertions by one `memmove()`, as `ArraySSet` does; the other
 realizations of the table above are the answers of chapters 2 and 3.

 `rotateArray()` (exercise 2.5) rotates an array by three reversals: with
 \f$ a = X Y \f$, \f$ |Y| = r \f$, the reversal of the whole gives
 \f$ Y^R X^R \f$, and reversing each part gives \f$ Y X \f$, i.e., each item
 \f$ r \f$ places on, with \f$ n \f$ swaps at most. `rotate()` on an
 `IndexedList` with no `rotate()` of its own follows instead the
 \f$ \gcd( n , r ) \f$ cycles of the permutation
 \f$ i \mapsto ( i + r ) \bmod n \f$, each item moved once, plus one move
 per cycle.

 \section theory_sequence_morin Sequences by rank and order maintenance

 Three sequences of Morin, Open Data Structures, reach an item by its
 rank in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected time: `SkipListSequence`, the
 `SkiplistList` of section 4.3, whose links carry their widths, the number
 of items they jump over; `TreapList`, the list on a treap of exercise
 7.11, whose nodes carry the sizes of their subtrees; and, for the order
 between two items rather than the access by rank, `OrderMaintenanceList`,
 the `Sequence` of exercise 8.11; the shuffle of exercise 7.4 closes the
 section.

 <em>Lemma 13 (the widths).</em> In `SkipListSequence`, if the link of each
 node \f$ x \f$ on the level \f$ l \f$ has the width
 \f$ \mathrm{index}( x.\mathit{next}[ l ] ) - \mathrm{index}( x ) \f$, the head
 having the index \f$ -1 \f$ and nil the index \f$ n \f$, then the search of
 the index \f$ i \f$ that sums the widths and goes right while the sum
 stays at most \f$ i \f$ ends at the item of index \f$ i \f$, in
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ expected time, and an insertion, a removal, a
 truncation and an absorption keep the widths in \f$ O( \log n ) \f$
 expected time.

 Indeed, the sum of the widths along the search is the index of the node
 reached, so the search is that of a skip list whose keys are the indices,
 and Lemma 7 of \ref theory_dictionary_tries bounds it. An insertion at
 \f$ i \f$ shifts by one the indices from \f$ i \f$ on: a link that passes
 over the new node grows by 1, and a link that the new node splits, from
 \f$ x \f$ of index \f$ j \f$, becomes two of widths \f$ i - j \f$ and the
 rest; the removal is the reverse. A truncation at \f$ i \f$ cuts on each
 level the link that crosses the index \f$ i \f$ and gives its end to the
 head of the new list, with the width reduced by \f$ i \f$; an absorption
 walks to the last node of each level, \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ steps in
 expectation since the walk is the search of the index \f$ n - 1 \f$, and
 links it to the first node of that level of the other list. The links to
 nil keep their widths, to the index \f$ n \f$, so that no level needs a
 special case.

 `TreapList` reads the rank of a node as the size of the left subtrees
 passed on the right along its path; its shape is that of a treap on the
 indices, a random binary search tree of expected depth \f$ O( \log n ) \f$
 whatever the order of the operations (Lemma 7.1 of Morin), and its split
 and join at an index are those of a treap on the keys.

 `OrderMaintenanceList` labels each node of a scapegoat tree by its path,
 so that `testBefore()` compares two words: the root has the label
 \f$ 2^{63} \f$ and the children of a node of label \f$ \ell \f$, whose lowest
 bit set is \f$ 2^j \f$, the labels \f$ \ell \pm 2^{j-1} \f$, which keeps the
 labels in symmetric order and holds a tree of height up to 63. The
 scapegoat tree with \f$ b = 3 / 2 \f$ has height at most
 \f$ \log_{3/2} q \f$, below 63 for fewer than \f$ 10^{11} \f$ nodes; a rebuild
 relabels the subtree it rebuilds in \f$ O( s ) \f$, inside the
 \f$ O( s ) \f$ of the rebuild, so that an insertion costs
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ amortized (Lemma 10 of \ref theory_tree_morin). A
 removal cannot splice its node, which would lift a subtree and change its
 labels, and marks it dead instead; the whole tree is rebuilt without the
 dead nodes when they are half of them, which costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ every
 \f$ n / 2 \f$ removals. Dietz and Sleator, and Bender et al. with a simpler
 relabeling, reach \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized insertions with labels of
 \f$ O( \log n ) \f$ bits; the scapegoat labels are the solution of the
 exercise, \f$ O( \log n ) \f$.

 <em>Lemma 14 (the shuffle, exercise 7.4 of Morin).</em> The shuffle of Fisher
 and Yates in the form of Durstenfeld, `shuffleFast()` [see
 `RandomGenerators.h`], gives each of the \f$ n! \f$ orders with probability
 \f$ 1 / n! \f$, in \f$ n - 1 \f$ swaps.

 Indeed, by induction on the places from the last: the item put in the place
 \f$ i \f$ is drawn uniformly among the \f$ i + 1 \f$ items of
 \f$ A[ 0 .. i ] \f$, independently of the draws for the places above
 \f$ i \f$, and each order is the outcome of exactly one sequence of draws,
 of probability \f$ \prod_{i=1}^{n-1} \frac{1}{i + 1} = \frac{1}{n!} \f$.
 The draw below \f$ m \f$ multiplies a word of 64 bits by \f$ m \f$ and takes
 the high word, rejecting the \f$ 2^{64} \bmod m \f$ values of the low word
 that would bias it (Lemire), which needs a division only when the low
 word is below \f$ m \f$, with probability \f$ m / 2^{64} \f$, and
 `shuffleFast()` draws several indices from one word when their product
 fits; the `shuffle()` of `RandomNumbers.h`, Algorithm 3.4.2P of Knuth with
 \f$ g() \bmod j \f$, carries the bias of the remainder.

